    "durationMs": 79384,
    "finalText": "The URL Shortener API is complete and fully verified against a live Postgres 16.\n\n**Implementation** — Express 4.21 + `pg` (node-postgres):\n- `src/app.js` — all routes: `POST /links` (201 with generated or custom 4–20 char `[A-Za-z0-9_-]` code, 409 on duplicates, 422 on invalid url/code/malformed JSON), `GET /links/:code`, `GET /r/:code` (302 + atomic `clicks = clicks + 1` in one UPDATE), and `GET /links` (ascending by id, with total). URLs are stored and returned exactly as submitted.\n- `migrations/` — plain SQL migrations applied by `run.js`, tracked in a `schema_migrations` table so re-runs are idempotent.\n- `src/db.js` — connection pool built from `DATABASE_URL` (with `PG*` env fallback), nothing hard-coded.\n- `start.sh` (executable) — runs pending migrations, then `exec`s the server in the foreground on `0.0.0.0:8000`.\n- `README.md` — includes the startup section.\n\n**Verified with curl against the real database**: 201/409/422/404 paths, redirect Location headers, click counts incrementing exactly once per redirect (0→1→2), and — after a full server restart — both the links and click counts survived while migrations correctly skipped already-applied files. Dependencies are installed, test data was truncated afterward, and port 8000 is free, so the suite can start clean with `sh start.sh`."
  }
